<h1>Horner's rule</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A polynomial A(x) = a<sub>0</sub> + a<sub>1</sub>x + a<sub>2</sub>x&sup2; + a<sub>3</sub>x&sup3; + &hellip; may be written as A(x) = a<sub>0</sub> + x(a<sub>1</sub> + x(a<sub>2</sub> + x(a<sub>3</sub> + &hellip;))).
</p>


<p>
<em>Note:
A polynomial may be evaluated at a point x', that is A(x') computed, in <a href="theta.html"><em>&Theta;(n)</em></a> time using Horner's rule.  That is, repeated multiplications and additions, rather than the naive methods of raising x to powers, multiplying by the coefficient, and accumulating.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

